  "reasons": {
    "added-deu-1-5": "Brenton's English has it with no Greek word of its own: it turns a Greek phrase into English ('came to pass' for ἐγενήθη; 'go', 'river', 'forth', 'thing', 'things', 'hand' of 'right hand', 'other' of 'other side', 'one' of 'one end'), or it renders one Greek word in two English words ('gold works' for Καταχρύσεα, 'hewn' rock for λαξευτήν, 'to witness' with 'I call' for διαμαρτύρομαι), or Swete's Greek lacks it (the 'rulers of fifties' of 1:15, 'Lord' where Swete has ὁ θεός alone in 2:14 and 4:34); each was read against its verse (mw-u2w5klk.349.11)."
